// =========================================================
// Torx Drive Sizes (ISO 10664)
// =========================================================
//
// A SECOND TABLE, KEYED BY T NUMBER. This one is not keyed by thread diameter
// and it must not be folded into the metric table: a drive size is not a
// property of a thread size. The PRODUCT standard pairs them, and sets how deep
// the recess goes — ISO 14579 and ISO 14583 both put a T20 in an M4, but the
// cap screw's is 1.42 to 1.80 deep and the pan head's 1.27 to 1.66 — so the
// pairing is a column per product standard in the metric table, and the recess
// itself is a row here.
//
// Columns: [T number, A across the lobes, B across the flutes].
//
// READ OFF ISO 10664:2014, Table 1, nominal dimensions, every socket number the
// standard has. The rows this table carried before that were not read off it
// were wrong in seven places — its T27 was the standard's T30, and everything
// above it had shifted — which is what the caveat that used to stand here was
// warning about.
//
// REFERENCES:
//   ISO 10664:2014  https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/63207/f79670cf0a214881aa40b4822580cce3/ISO-10664-2014.pdf
//   ISO 10664 (earlier edition, cross-check)
//                   https://willrich.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/11/ISO-10664_.pdf
torxTable = [[  1.0,  0.9,  0.6],
             [  2.0,  1.0,  0.7],
             [  3.0,  1.2, 0.85],
             [  4.0, 1.35,  1.0],
             [  5.0,  1.5,  1.1],
             [  6.0, 1.75, 1.27],
             [  7.0,  2.1,  1.5],
             [  8.0,  2.4, 1.75],
             [  9.0,  2.6,  1.9],
             [ 10.0,  2.8, 2.05],
             [ 15.0, 3.35,  2.4],
             [ 20.0, 3.95, 2.85],
             [ 25.0,  4.5, 3.25],
             [ 27.0,  5.1, 3.68],
             [ 30.0,  5.6, 4.05],
             [ 40.0, 6.75, 4.85],
             [ 45.0, 7.93, 5.64],
             [ 50.0, 8.95, 6.45],
             [ 55.0, 11.35, 8.05],
             [ 60.0, 13.45,  9.6],
             [ 70.0, 15.7, 11.2],
             [ 80.0, 17.75, 12.8],
             [ 90.0, 20.2, 14.4],
             [100.0, 22.4, 16.0]];

/// The A dimension of a Torx size — the diameter ACROSS THE LOBES, which is
/// what a T number is quoted by. catalog.torxMajor(25) is a T25.
///
/// Zero for a T number this table has no row for, the same as every other
/// lookup here.
torxMajor(tNumber) {
  k = tNumber;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[1] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    torxTable));
}

/// The B dimension of a Torx size — the diameter across the flutes between the
/// lobes.
torxMinor(tNumber) {
  k = tNumber;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[2] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    torxTable));
}

// =========================================================
// Cross Recess Sizes (ISO 4757)
// =========================================================
//
// KEYED BY RECESS NUMBER, like the Torx table and for the same reason: the
// number is the drive's own size, and which number a screw carries is a column
// of its product standard (panRecess for ISO 7045).
//
// WHAT ISO 4757 DOES NOT GIVE is the part that depends on the screw. It
// tabulates the FORM of each recess number; the diameter m the recess opens to
// at the face and the depth its gauge penetrates are in the product standard,
// per thread size, because the same PH2 is sunk shallower into a thin head than
// a deep one. So a recess is a row here AND two columns there, and a drawing
// needs both.
//
// A TYPE H RECESS IS FOUR RIBS LEFT STANDING IN A CONE, and its letters are
// the ribs, not the wings. Each rib is the metal between two wings: its ridge
// points at the axis and leans out at beta going up, the ridges of opposite
// ribs are b apart where they meet the bottom, and across each ridge the rib is
// a blunt alpha facet e wide, then flanks 92 degrees apart — both angles in the
// section square to the ridge. The cone around them leans out at 26 degrees 30
// from the axis and meets the bottom — a 28 degree cone to a point — at
// diameter g, t1 = g/2 tan 28 above the point. The wing thickness f is not an
// input: it is what the ribs leave between them, and the construction gives
// Table 1's f back (drill.phillipsAt).
//
// A recess number is one form sunk to the depth each screw's m puts it at: the
// cone crosses the reference plane at m, so a larger m sinks the same form
// deeper. The gauge penetration is then a CHECK on the form rather than its
// depth: ISO's own gauge, seated in the recess, has to reach between the
// product standard's minimum and maximum.
//
// JIS B 1012 IS THIS TABLE. It declares its types H and Z conformant with ISO
// 4757-1983, and its own Table 2 matches type H here in every angle and in b,
// e and g for recess numbers 1 to 4. No. 0 differs a little — b 0.605 against
// 0.61, and a radius where ISO has two small faces between the wing walls —
// so a JIS No. 0 is the one JIS recess this table does not draw. ASME
// B18.6.3's Type I is this table too: its gauge (Appendix III) agrees with
// ISO's Table 2 dimension for dimension for Nos. 1 to 4, and differs at No. 0.
//
// REFERENCES:
//   JIS B 1012:1985 https://kikakurui.com/b1/B1012-1985-01.html
//   JIS B 1111:2017 https://kikakurui.com/b1/B1111-2017-01.html
//   ASME B18.6.3-2010, Appendix III, read from an unofficial copy:
//                   https://pdfcoffee.com/asme-b1863-2010-pdf-free.html
//
// Read off ISO 4757:1983. Type H (Phillips), Table 1, columns:
//   0  recess number
//   1  b, core width across the rib ridges at the bottom
//   2  e, width of each ridge's alpha facet — the middle of Table 1's range
//   3  g, diameter where the wing-end cone meets the bottom
//   4  alpha, across each ridge, DEGREES
//   5  beta, the lean of each ridge off the axis, DEGREES
//   6  root radius — No. 0 only
// NO. 0 HAS NO ALPHA. Table 1 replaces its facets with a radius, 0.25 to 0.36;
// the radius is taken at 0.25, where it meets the flanks 2 r cos 46 = 0.347
// apart — inside Table 1's e for No. 0, so e there is a consequence and its
// column is not read. The 92 degree flanks, 26 degree 30 wing ends and 28
// degree bottom are every size's and are literals in drill.
phillipsTable = [[0.0, 0.61, 0.31,  0.81,   0.0, 7.0,  0.25],
                 [1.0, 0.97, 0.435, 1.27, 138.0, 7.0,  0.0],
                 [2.0, 1.47, 0.815, 2.29, 140.0, 5.75, 0.0],
                 [3.0, 2.41, 2.005, 3.81, 146.0, 5.75, 0.0],
                 [4.0, 3.48, 2.415, 5.08, 153.0, 7.0,  0.0]];

// ISO 4757:1983 Table 2, the type H GAUGE: the point of the matching
// screwdriver, which is what a recess is checked with. Columns as the recess
// table, with the gauge's own flat end d added:
//   [recess number, b, e, g, d, alpha, beta, root radius]
// Each is the nominal Table 2 prints, the largest gauge its tolerances allow
// on b and e and the smallest on g. No. 0 is again a radius, 0.25 +/- 0.025;
// taken at 0.225, the one radius that also puts the flanks at Table 2's e.
phillipsGaugeTable = [[0.0, 0.64,  0.315, 0.813, 0.25,   0.0, 7.0,  0.225],
                      [1.0, 1.001, 0.513, 1.27,  0.38, 138.0, 7.0,  0.0],
                      [2.0, 1.539, 1.102, 2.286, 0.38, 140.0, 5.75, 0.0],
                      [3.0, 2.497, 2.098, 3.81,  0.38, 146.0, 5.75, 0.0],
                      [4.0, 3.574, 2.738, 5.08,  0.38, 153.0, 7.0,  0.0]];

// REFERENCES for both type H tables and the construction drill.phillipsAt
// draws from them:
//   ISO 4757:1983   https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/10742/d878fa0371e041b39de75de4d4426928/ISO-4757-1983.pdf
//                   (Tables 1 and 2 and the type H figures)
//   EN ISO 4757:1994, the same tables:
//                   http://www.f0512.com/wp-content/uploads/ISO4757.pdf
//   FreeCAD Fasteners' reading of the same drawing (makeHCrossRecess),
//   against which the rib construction was checked:
//                   https://raw.githubusercontent.com/shaise/FreeCAD_FastenersWB/master/screw_maker.py

// Type Z (Pozidriv), Table 3. A TYPE Z RECESS IS A CROSS WITH A SQUARE CORE:
// four wings f wide on parallel walls, and between them a core whose four
// corners run out along the diagonals into V-shaped VALLEYS, b apart across
// the diagonal. The wings' floors are the 26 degree 30 cone, which meets the
// 28 degree bottom at the KNEE, diameter g — b, f and g are all measured at
// the knee plane. Each valley's floor leans out at alpha going up; its two
// walls meet the wings' walls at 46 degrees plus delta of metal, 92 degrees
// for Nos. 0 to 2 and 102 degrees 15 for 3 and 4. j is the depth of the four
// marks stamped along the diagonals, which ISO draws with the recess. Columns:
//   [recess number, b, f, g, alpha DEGREES, delta DEGREES, j]
//
// NOT MODELLED: beta and gamma, which ASME B18.6.3 calls "milling offset"
// angles and which tilt the valley and wing walls a few tenths of a degree
// (ASME's derived wing offsets N and N1); r1, the lead-in radius at the head
// surface; r2, the round on the valley edges.
//
// This table used to read b as the wing width, g as a round hub, gamma as the
// wing wall taper and the marks as ribs; the drawing, the gauge and the
// patent say otherwise.
//
// REFERENCES:
//   ISO 4757:1983   https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/10742/d878fa0371e041b39de75de4d4426928/ISO-4757-1983.pdf
//                   (Table 3 and the type Z figures)
//   IS 7478:2011, India's verbatim adoption, with the type Z gauge Table 4
//   the ISO sample lacks and an unwatermarked drawing:
//                   https://archive.org/details/gov.in.is.7478.2011
//   US 3,237,506, the Pozidriv patent, whose Fig. 3 draws the valleys:
//                   https://patents.google.com/patent/US3237506A/en
//   ASME B18.6.3-2010 Table III-3, the Type IA gauge, unofficial copy:
//                   https://pdfcoffee.com/asme-b1863-2010-pdf-free.html
pozidrivTable = [[0.0, 0.76, 0.48, 0.86, 7.0,  46.0,  0.13],
                 [1.0, 1.27, 0.74, 1.32, 7.0,  46.0,  0.15],
                 [2.0, 1.83, 1.03, 2.34, 5.75, 46.0,  0.15],
                 [3.0, 2.72, 1.42, 3.86, 5.75, 56.25, 0.20],
                 [4.0, 3.96, 2.16, 5.08, 7.0,  56.25, 0.20]];

// ISO 4757 Table 4, the type Z GAUGE, read from IS 7478: [recess number, b,
// f, g, p]. b and f at the top of their ranges and g at the bottom, the
// largest gauge; p, the axial distance from the gauge's g plane to its point,
// in the middle of its range. alpha and delta are Table 3's.
pozidrivGaugeTable = [[0.0, 0.711, 0.445, 0.890, 0.0705],
                      [1.0, 1.112, 0.698, 1.372, 0.1595],
                      [2.0, 1.702, 0.990, 2.413, 0.3245],
                      [3.0, 2.591, 1.372, 3.937, 0.5785],
                      [4.0, 3.861, 2.083, 5.157, 0.7815]];

/// ISO 4757 type H (Phillips) core width b for a recess number: across the
/// ridges of opposite ribs, where they meet the bottom —
/// catalog.phillipsCore(2.0) is 1.47, a PH2. NOT a wing width; a PH2's wing is
/// about 0.7 across.
phillipsCore(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[1] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    phillipsTable));
}

/// ISO 4757 type H facet width e: how wide the blunt alpha facet on each rib's
/// ridge is, the middle of Table 1's range.
phillipsFacet(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[2] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    phillipsTable));
}

/// ISO 4757 type H point diameter g: where the 26 degree 30 wing-end cone
/// meets the recess bottom.
phillipsPoint(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[3] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    phillipsTable));
}

/// ISO 4757 type H angle alpha across each rib's ridge, in RADIANS; zero for
/// No. 0, whose ridge is a radius — see phillipsRoot.
phillipsRidge(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return radians(reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                        map((r) { return r[4] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                            phillipsTable)));
}

/// ISO 4757 type H ridge lean beta, in RADIANS: 7 degrees for PH0, PH1 and
/// PH4, 5 degrees 45 minutes for PH2 and PH3. Each rib's ridge leans out by it
/// going up, so the ribs close on a driver as it goes in — which is what makes
/// a Phillips driver ride up out of the recess under torque.
phillipsFlank(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return radians(reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                        map((r) { return r[5] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                            phillipsTable)));
}

/// ISO 4757 type H ridge radius: No. 0's in place of the alpha facets, zero
/// for every other number.
phillipsRoot(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[6] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    phillipsTable));
}

/// The type H GAUGE's core width b — ISO 4757 Table 2.
phillipsGaugeCore(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[1] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    phillipsGaugeTable));
}

/// The type H gauge's facet width e.
phillipsGaugeFacet(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[2] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    phillipsGaugeTable));
}

/// The type H gauge's point diameter g, where its wing ends meet its end.
phillipsGaugePoint(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[3] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    phillipsGaugeTable));
}

/// The type H gauge's flat end diameter d.
phillipsGaugeFlat(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[4] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    phillipsGaugeTable));
}

/// The type H gauge's ridge angle alpha, in RADIANS; zero for No. 0.
phillipsGaugeRidge(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return radians(reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                        map((r) { return r[5] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                            phillipsGaugeTable)));
}

/// The type H gauge's ridge lean beta, in RADIANS.
phillipsGaugeFlank(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return radians(reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                        map((r) { return r[6] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                            phillipsGaugeTable)));
}

/// The type H gauge's ridge radius: No. 0's, zero for every other number.
phillipsGaugeRoot(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[7] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    phillipsGaugeTable));
}

/// ISO 4757 type Z (Pozidriv) WING WIDTH f for a recess number: across a
/// wing's parallel walls, at the knee — catalog.pozidrivWing(2.0) is 1.03.
pozidrivWing(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[2] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    pozidrivTable));
}

/// ISO 4757 type Z core width b: across opposite valleys, along the
/// diagonal, at the knee.
pozidrivCore(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[1] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    pozidrivTable));
}

/// ISO 4757 type Z knee diameter g: where the 26 degree 30 wing floors meet
/// the 28 degree bottom.
pozidrivKnee(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[3] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    pozidrivTable));
}

/// ISO 4757 type Z valley lean alpha, in RADIANS: how far each valley's floor
/// leans out from the axis going up.
pozidrivLean(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return radians(reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                        map((r) { return r[4] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                            pozidrivTable)));
}

/// ISO 4757 type Z delta, in RADIANS: with the fixed 46 degrees, the metal
/// angle where a valley wall meets a wing wall.
pozidrivValley(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return radians(reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                        map((r) { return r[5] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                            pozidrivTable)));
}

/// ISO 4757 type Z mark depth j: how deep the four marks along the diagonals
/// start, at their inner ends.
pozidrivMarkDepth(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[6] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    pozidrivTable));
}

/// The type Z GAUGE's core width b — ISO 4757 Table 4.
pozidrivGaugeCore(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[1] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    pozidrivGaugeTable));
}

/// The type Z gauge's wing width f.
pozidrivGaugeWing(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[2] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    pozidrivGaugeTable));
}

/// The type Z gauge's knee diameter g.
pozidrivGaugeKnee(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[3] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    pozidrivGaugeTable));
}

/// The type Z gauge's p: from its g plane down to its point.
pozidrivGaugePoint(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[4] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    pozidrivGaugeTable));
}

// =========================================================
// Square Recess Sizes (Robertson)
// =========================================================
//
// KEYED BY SIZE NUMBER, #0 to #4. There is no ISO standard for the square
// recess; ASME B18.6.3 carries it as the Type III square centre, and it pairs
// with inch screws — the Inch Product Standards below say which screw takes
// which size, and how deep. #00 exists and is left out: a size number cannot be
// written 00 as a key, and nothing in this library is small enough to want it.
//
// Columns: [size number, across flats at the face, recess depth].
//
// VERIFY BEFORE TRUSTING A VALUE. The across-flats figures are the middle of
// the ranges a manufacturer's summary of ASME B18.6.3 gives; the depths are from
// a second summary, and the two disagree on depth by up to half a millimetre at
// #1 and #3. Neither was read off the standard itself.
//
// REFERENCES — manufacturer and secondary summaries, not ASME B18.6.3 itself:
//   across flats   https://www.aspenfasteners.com/content/pdf/square_drive_specification.pdf
//   depths         https://boltingspecialist.com/dimensions/asme-b18-6-3-combination-slotted-type-3-square-recessed-truss-head-machine-screws/index.html
//   (secondary)    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robertson_screw — its ranges agree with Aspen's
robertsonTable = [[0.0, 1.786, 1.68],
                  [1.0, 2.299, 2.44],
                  [2.0, 2.840, 2.92],
                  [3.0, 3.359, 3.63],
                  [4.0, 4.832, 4.93]];

/// A Robertson (square) recess's width across flats at the face:
/// catalog.robertsonAcross(2.0) is a #2, the red one.
robertsonAcross(sizeNo) {
  k = sizeNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[1] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    robertsonTable));
}

/// A Robertson recess's depth, to its deepest point.
robertsonDepth(sizeNo) {
  k = sizeNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[2] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    robertsonTable));
}

// =========================================================
// Inch Product Standards (ASME B18.6.3)
// =========================================================
//
// KEYED BY NOMINAL DIAMETER IN INCHES — 0.164 is a #8, 0.25 a 1/4" — the key
// thread.uncPitch reads, so an inch screw's thread and its head are looked up
// by the same number. Every dimension is in MILLIMETRES, like the rest of the
// library: the tables are inches times 25.4. Only the sizes the square recess
// is tabulated for are here, #4 to 1/4"; a #2 pan head has no Type III row at
// all, and the sources disagree on which recess a #2 flat head takes.
//
// A SQUARE RECESS HAS TWO DEPTHS PER SIZE. The standard gives a #1 and a #2 a
// short and a regular depth (1S, 1R, 2S, 2R), and which a screw gets is its own
// column — a #6 pan head takes 1R, a #6 flat head 1S. So a product row carries
// its recess number, for the width across flats, AND its depth; robertsonDepth
// is the short one.
//
// VERIFY BEFORE TRUSTING A VALUE. These were read off distributor reproductions
// of ASME B18.6.3 (Bolting Specialist, RC Fasteners, and Industrial Threaded
// Products for the depths), not the standard itself; heads are their maxima,
// depths the reference T.
//
// REFERENCES:
//   Type III pan   https://boltingspecialist.com/dimensions/asme-b18-6-3-type-3-square-recessed-pan-head-machine-screws/index.html
//   Type III flat  https://boltingspecialist.com/dimensions/asme-b18-6-3-type-3-square-recess-flat-countersunk-head-machine-screws/index.html
//   recess table   https://boltingspecialist.com/dimensions/asme-b18-6-3-recess-flat-countersunk-head-screws/index.html
//   RC Fasteners   https://www.rcfastener.com/documents/RC_Specs/Machine_Screw_Square_Flat_Pan_Hd_Dimensions.pdf
//   depths (ITP)   https://www.buildsite.com/pdf/industrialthreadedproducts/Self-Tapping-Screws-Product-Data-2101138.pdf
//   cross-checks   https://boltingspecialist.com/dimensions/asme-b18-6-3-slotted-flat-countersunk-head-screws/index.html
//                  https://boltingspecialist.com/dimensions/asme-b18-6-3-type-1-cross-recessed-pan-head-machine-screws/index.html
//                  https://www.aspenfasteners.com/content/pdf/square_drive_specification.pdf
//                  https://obriensupply.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/Flat-Head-82-100-Degree-MS.pdf
//                  https://www.aftfasteners.com/82-degree-flat-head-machine-screws-dimensions-specifications/
//                  https://www.electronicfasteners.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/headdimensionsmachinescrews.pdf
//                  https://marathonhardware.com/brochures/catalogues/5.fasteners/2015/f.-fasteners.pdf
//                  https://www.brightonbest.com/download/pds/PDS_Thread_Details_Inch_Series.pdf
//                  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robertson_screw
//   ASME preview   https://files.asme.org/Catalog/Codes/PrintBook/28994.pdf

// Type III square recessed PAN HEAD machine screw. The standard dimensions the
// head's diameter, its height and the radius of its top edge, and not the
// crown, so the head is drawn flat-topped with that edge. Columns:
//   0  nominal diameter, inches
//   1  head diameter A (max)
//   2  head height H (max)
//   3  top edge radius R (min)
//   4  square recess size number
//   5  square recess depth T
panB1863 = [[0.1120,  5.563, 2.184, 0.254, 0.0, 1.676],
            [0.1380,  6.858, 2.616, 0.381, 1.0, 2.692],
            [0.1640,  8.179, 3.048, 0.381, 2.0, 3.226],
            [0.1900,  9.474, 3.480, 0.508, 2.0, 3.226],
            [0.2500, 12.497, 4.445, 0.889, 3.0, 4.013]];

// Type III square recessed 82-degree FLAT HEAD machine screw. The head is the
// cone from A down to the shank, so its height is not a column: the reference
// height the tables print does not agree with their own maximum A. Columns:
//   0  nominal diameter, inches
//   1  head diameter A, the theoretical sharp edge (max)
//   2  square recess size number
//   3  square recess depth T
flatB1863 = [[0.1120,  5.385, 0.0, 1.676],
             [0.1380,  6.655, 1.0, 2.438],
             [0.1640,  7.925, 2.0, 2.921],
             [0.1900,  9.195, 2.0, 3.226],
             [0.2500, 12.116, 3.0, 4.013]];

/// ASME B18.6.3 square recessed pan head diameter A, in millimetres, for a
/// nominal diameter in INCHES: catalog.inchPanHeadDia(0.164) is a #8's. Zero
/// outside #4 to 1/4".
inchPanHeadDia(nominalInch) {
  k = nominalInch;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[1] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    panB1863));
}

/// ASME B18.6.3 pan head height H, in millimetres.
inchPanHeadHeight(nominalInch) {
  k = nominalInch;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[2] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    panB1863));
}

/// The radius of an ASME B18.6.3 pan head's top edge, in millimetres.
inchPanEdge(nominalInch) {
  k = nominalInch;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[3] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    panB1863));
}

/// The square recess size an ASME B18.6.3 pan head takes: 0 for a #4, 1 for a
/// #6, 2 for a #8 and a #10, 3 for a 1/4". Zero is a size, so ask
/// inchPanHeadDia whether the screw exists.
inchPanRecess(nominalInch) {
  k = nominalInch;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[4] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    panB1863));
}

/// The depth of an ASME B18.6.3 pan head's square recess, in millimetres —
/// the regular depth, where its size has two.
inchPanRecessDepth(nominalInch) {
  k = nominalInch;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[5] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    panB1863));
}

/// ASME B18.6.3 82-degree flat head diameter A, the theoretical sharp edge, in
/// millimetres, for a nominal diameter in INCHES. Zero outside #4 to 1/4".
inchFlatHeadDia(nominalInch) {
  k = nominalInch;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[1] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    flatB1863));
}

/// The square recess size an ASME B18.6.3 flat head takes.
inchFlatRecess(nominalInch) {
  k = nominalInch;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[2] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    flatB1863));
}

/// The depth of an ASME B18.6.3 flat head's square recess, in millimetres —
/// the short depth for a #6 and a #8, which is what their thinner heads leave
/// room for.
inchFlatRecessDepth(nominalInch) {
  k = nominalInch;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[3] * onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    flatB1863));
}
